    "result_text": "Creating an owner now returns **429 Too Many Requests** once 100 or more owners already have today's registration date. I ran `./mvnw test` and it passed. There is only one test in the suite, `AcceptanceTest.coreRejectsOverDailyLimit`: it creates 100 owners and expects the 101st to be rejected, and it passes.\n\n**What changed:**\n- **Controller** (`OwnerRestControllerV1.addOwner`): added a `DAILY_OWNER_LIMIT = 100` constant. It counts owners registered on `LocalDate.now()` and throws a new `DailyOwnerLimitExceededException` when the count is at or above the limit. This check runs after the existing duplicate and city-capacity checks, so those still return 409 first.\n- **Error response**: a new handler in `ExceptionControllerAdvice` turns that exception into a 429 error body, with a `limit` property alongside the standard fields.\n- **Service and repository**: added `ClinicService.countOwnersRegisteredOn(LocalDate)`, which uses a new `OwnerRepository.countByRegistrationDate`. I implemented that for all three storage backends (JPA, JDBC and Spring Data JPA).\n- **API spec**: `openapi.yml` now lists a 429 response for `POST /owners`.\n\n**Things to know:**\n- The test only exercises the JPA backend. The JDBC and Spring Data versions compile but no test runs them.\n- \"Today\" means the server's local date.\n- An owner created with an explicit registration date that isn't today still counts toward that other day's total, not today's.\n- The check isn't atomic: if requests arrive at the same time right at the limit, slightly more than 100 owners could get through. That's the same as the existing city-capacity check.",
